---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import RoverAnatomy from "@/components/rover/RoverAnatomy.astro";
import RoverDemo from "@/components/rover/RoverDemo.astro";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Rover path planner"
  description="An interactive 3D lunar rover on unmapped terrain that maps its way with stereo cameras and an IMU, reroutes around hazards and backs out of trouble."
  wide
>
  <header class="mb-8 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="label">Personal project</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink md:text-4xl">
      Rover path planner
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-pretty text-ink-muted">
      A lunar rover dropped onto ground nobody has mapped. It builds a map from
      the 3D points its stereo cameras measure, plans across it, reroutes when
      the map changes, and backs out of trouble when it gets it wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-faint">
      Built in my own time; it isn't based on my work at Lockheed Martin.
    </p>
  </header>

  <RoverDemo />

  <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-faint">
    Click anywhere on the terrain, or on the map in the corner, to send the
    rover there. Drag to slide the view around the map, right-drag or Shift-drag
    to orbit, scroll to zoom; Follow brings the camera back to the rover. On a
    touch screen, tap to send it and use two fingers to orbit and zoom.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
      Inside the rover
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-2 mb-5 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      A detailed model of the simulated rover: its cameras, flight computer,
      motor controllers, actuators and the harness that ties them together.
      Hover a part or a wire to see what it does, why it's built that way and
      what it's wired to; the wires that serve it light up.
    </p>
    <RoverAnatomy />
  </section>

  <article class="prose-note mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2>How it works</h2>

    <h3>Seeing in 3D</h3>
    <p>
      The rover has no map to start with, only the edge of its operating area,
      and it assumes unknown ground is probably drivable. That optimism is what
      lets it plan at all, and also why it has to replan so often.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything it learns comes from three stereo camera pairs. The pair on the
      mast pans to look along the route and sweeps from side to side while the
      rover waits. Wide-angle pairs low on the front and back cover the ground
      around the wheels, which the mast can't see past the body. Each frame
      becomes a cloud of 3D points. The simulation makes the points by casting
      rays against the real surface and adding the error a stereo pair really
      has: it grows with the square of distance, so far points are much shakier
      than near ones. Turn on Sensors to see each camera's view traced on the
      ground and the points streaming in.
    </p>
    <p>
      The points fall into a one-metre elevation map. For each cell the rover
      estimates the ground height from its lowest points, the tilt across its
      wheelbase from the cells around it, how bumpy the ground is, and whether
      something stands up out of it, like a rock. It never sees the true
      terrain, only these estimates, so the back of a boulder stays unknown
      until it drives past, and far ground can be misjudged until it gets
      closer.
    </p>

    <h3>Knowing its own limits</h3>
    <p>
      One vehicle description drives everything: a 3 by 2.2 metre body, wheels
      that can climb about 34 centimetres, a 20° limit on the slopes it will
      choose, and 27° before it risks tipping. Ground past a limit is no-go,
      hatched on the heat map. That is where a wheel must never go. Around every
      no-go cell there is a keep-out ring about half a body wide, where the
      centre of the rover can't go because its side would be over the hazard.
      Beyond that is a ring where a turn could swing a corner over it, which is
      costly but allowed. Steps are flagged a little below the true limit, as
      the estimates are noisy and a missed rock costs far more than a detour.
    </p>
    <p>
      An IMU reports the body's roll and pitch, shown under the status. The
      rover slows on slopes it can feel. If the tilt nears its limit it stops
      and backs off, whatever the map said.
    </p>

    <h3>Planning</h3>
    <p>
      Routes come from A* on an 8-connected one-metre grid, then get
      straightened wherever a direct line is clear and no more costly. The
      search runs in a Web Worker so it never stalls the animation. When the
      cameras find a hazard on the route, the old path flashes orange and a new
      one is planned; smaller changes are folded in quietly while it keeps
      driving. The cost map updates only the cells new points touch.
    </p>

    <h3>Staying out of no-go zones</h3>
    <p>
      Every route is checked against the latest map before the rover drives it,
      so a plan worked out before a new hazard appeared is thrown away rather
      than followed. While driving it refuses any move that takes its centre
      into a keep-out ring or puts a wheel on no-go ground it knows about. When
      that happens it follows the grid route closely for a few metres instead of
      cutting corners. If it finds itself inside a keep-out ring, because a
      hazard was only just seen, it picks its way out along the direction that
      gets clear fastest.
    </p>

    <h3>When things go wrong</h3>
    <p>
      The body also checks its footprint against the real ground before every
      move, which catches what the cameras got wrong. If it touches a rock, the
      tilt gets too steep, or it stops making progress, it marks the spot as
      no-go, reverses along its own tracks (the one path it knows is clear), and
      replans. After four failed recoveries in a row, or if no route exists
      through what it knows, it stops and says it is stuck. Some places are
      simply stuck.
    </p>

    <h3>What's simplified</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Stereo depth is simulated by ray casting with range-dependent noise, not
        by matching real images, and the rover always knows exactly where it is.
        A real rover would also estimate its own position from wheel odometry,
        the IMU and visual odometry, and that estimate would drift.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every replan searches from scratch. That is fine at this size; an
        incremental planner like D* Lite would reuse earlier work on larger
        maps.
      </li>
      <li>
        The suspension is animated to follow the ground, not simulated as a
        mechanism.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The terrain is a generated lunar surface: rolling regolith and a power-law
      crater field, lit by a low sun as near the poles. Lighting and shadows for
      the ground are computed once in a Web Worker along with the terrain, so
      the page only draws; the rover is the one thing with a live shadow. It is
      a toy built from textbook methods and a generic design, and none of it
      downloads until you reach for the Run button or scroll to the model.
    </p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
